WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:05.422
The US gun market is
the biggest in the world by far.

00:00:05.422 --> 00:00:07.841
Gun sales across the United States

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.134
have reached record highs.

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:11.302
We produce the most guns.

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:16.891
And as the market in the US
gets to some point of saturation,

00:00:16.891 --> 00:00:17.350
in other words,

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.061 position:59%
they've already sold 400 million guns.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:22.981
There's been a push by

00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:26.234 position:56%
US gun manufacturers to sell abroad.

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:29.362
The problem is that

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:30.989
the places that want guns

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:32.991 position:67%
tend to be countries with high-crime rates,

00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:35.368
a lot of political instability,

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:36.286
or a lot of violence.

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:48.840
There are now more guns
than people in the US.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.800 position:59%
And even though American gun purchases

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.052
haven't slowed down,

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:53.762
manufacturers have set their sights

00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:56.514 position:59%
outside the US to boost sales as well.

00:00:57.932 --> 00:00:59.934
You go back almost 20 years,

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:02.812 position:58%
the assault rifle ban expires in 2004

00:01:02.812 --> 00:01:04.898
and that pushes gun manufacturers

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:06.983 position:59%
to start to develop new lines of guns,

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:09.527
including AR-15 style guns.

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:13.823
At the same time, the gun industry

00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:15.742
and individual gun companies

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:18.244 position:64%
start trying to tap international markets

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:19.579
as a way to grow more sales.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.832
And so the US manufacturers

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:24.084
they've used both,

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:26.628
their own money, their own power.

00:01:26.628 --> 00:01:29.047 position:64%
They've used advocacy groups like the NRA

00:01:29.047 --> 00:01:31.508
to help open markets for the US

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:32.926
and to beat back

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:34.886
gun control in other countries.

00:01:34.886 --> 00:01:36.471
And they've also used the power

00:01:36.471 --> 00:01:37.639
of the US government.

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:42.811
A regulatory change begun
under the Obama administration

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:45.772
and completed by the
Trump administration in 2020

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:48.775
gave gun manufacturers
the greater freedom to export

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:50.026
that they'd been looking for.

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:53.404 position:58%
They said, look we’re regulating guns

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:56.282 position:66%
and a lot of what they call dual-use items

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:59.119
the same as you would
regulate weapons of war.

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:01.037
And in some cases it made sense.

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:02.872 position:59%
You shouldn't have to get a license to

00:02:02.872 --> 00:02:06.042 position:67%
sell a screw that might be used in an F-16.

00:02:06.042 --> 00:02:07.794
They loosened the restrictions

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:10.088
and instead of it going
through the State Department,

00:02:10.088 --> 00:02:12.132
it's now handled under
the Commerce Department.

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:16.010 position:56%
The idea is that guns,
just like any other US made product,

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:19.681
Commerce sees part of its role
is to help sell those things.

00:02:19.681 --> 00:02:21.558
They do that with planes,
they do that with whiskey,

00:02:21.558 --> 00:02:23.309 position:59%
they do it with all sorts of products.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.645 position:66%
But when they did this, they also included

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:26.771
very controversial weapons

00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:29.440
the AR-15, the 50-caliber guns

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.568 position:56%
that the cartels are using in Mexico

00:02:31.568 --> 00:02:33.987 position:56%
and can shoot through engine blocks.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:38.741
Those are now
treated as commercial items.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.246
So where do these guns end up?

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:45.790
It's probably important to note
that most countries in the world

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.584
don't want to buy US guns

00:02:47.584 --> 00:02:48.918
because most countries in the world

00:02:48.918 --> 00:02:50.753
have relatively strict gun laws.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:52.881
And so you have this mix of

00:02:52.881 --> 00:02:54.424
US guns going to places

00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:55.466
with a lot of instability

00:02:55.466 --> 00:02:57.302 position:63%
and not the strongest hold on democracy.

00:02:58.636 --> 00:03:01.306 position:63%
Tracking gun shipments is very difficult

00:03:01.306 --> 00:03:03.725 position:58%
because they don't put much data out.

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:04.601
And what they put out

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:06.811
is very complicated.

00:03:07.103 --> 00:03:09.689
You need a license
to export guns from the US.

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:10.690
In order to get the license,

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:11.733
you have to have approval

00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:13.151
of the country where

00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:14.736
the weapons are going to go.

00:03:14.736 --> 00:03:17.197
Where the guns go once they
end up in those countries,

00:03:17.197 --> 00:03:19.866
are a total different matter.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.327
In Mexico, there's one gun store

00:03:22.327 --> 00:03:24.037 position:64%
and there's hundreds of thousands of guns

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:26.122 position:67%
that go to the Mexican police and military.

00:03:26.122 --> 00:03:27.749 position:56%
But a lot of those guns later end up

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:31.211 position:73%
in the hands of cartels or paramilitary groups.

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:34.422
In Thailand, there's pretty strict
gun control on the record.

00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:36.633 position:58%
But the black market is so widespread

00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:38.551
that people advertise on Facebook

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.511
and Twitter to sell guns illegally.

00:03:41.429 --> 00:03:42.639 position:61%
Over the last few years there have been

00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:45.350
all-time highs in US gun exports.

00:03:50.271 --> 00:03:51.397
One of the most troubling things

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:54.150 position:63%
is that there is an increase in violence

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.654 position:66%
where a lot of the exports are increasing.

00:03:57.654 --> 00:03:59.030
And you got to be careful,

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:00.782
correlation is not causation,

00:04:00.782 --> 00:04:03.701 position:59%
but in some places like Latin America,

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:06.412 position:64%
they say that about 20% of the crime guns

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:09.040
recovered were legally exported.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.251
If you talk to people
in Guatemala and Honduras,

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:13.253 position:56%
they say a lot of the migrant crisis

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:15.964 position:69%
at the US border is being fueled by the fact

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:17.966 position:61%
that there's just rampant street crimes

00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:20.051
and cartels and drug violence

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:21.302
and there's so many guns there.

00:04:21.302 --> 00:04:22.345
And the US is sending more guns.

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.514
In Mexico, for instance,

00:04:24.514 --> 00:04:26.474
they say less than 2% of crime guns

00:04:26.474 --> 00:04:28.101
are legally from the US.

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:32.063
But Mexico only submits
a very small portion of overall

00:04:32.063 --> 00:04:33.356
crime guns to be traced.

00:04:33.356 --> 00:04:35.733 position:61%
But it is clear that significant number

00:04:35.733 --> 00:04:38.111 position:56%
of the crime guns in other countries

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:40.655 position:59%
are legal exports rather than illegal.

00:04:42.282 --> 00:04:45.201
Along with guns,
manufacturers are doing their best

00:04:45.201 --> 00:04:48.037 position:66%
to spread the culture around guns as well.

00:04:48.037 --> 00:04:50.331 position:67%
In order to sell more guns internationally,

00:04:50.331 --> 00:04:52.834
these gun manufacturers, gun lobby,

00:04:52.834 --> 00:04:55.044
they want to export
American gun culture as well.

00:04:55.044 --> 00:04:57.630
Which is, owning a gun is a right

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.966
to as violence increases in places

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:01.301
like Central America and elsewhere,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.219 position:66%
that the best way to counter that violence

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:04.971
is to arm ordinary people

00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:06.222
so that they can defend themselves.

00:05:07.223 --> 00:05:08.516
Sig Sauer, for example,

00:05:08.516 --> 00:05:10.059 position:58%
has something called the Sig Academy.

00:05:10.059 --> 00:05:11.477
They will fly people over

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.980
and let them try a lot
of different guns, shoot guns.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.108 position:56%
At the same time, they're in the US,
they travel business class.

00:05:17.108 --> 00:05:18.234
It's a nice trip.

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:19.694 position:58%
They'll also do a lot of sponsorships

00:05:19.694 --> 00:05:21.279
for police departments for events

00:05:21.279 --> 00:05:23.031 position:64%
and that creates a sort of a relationship

00:05:23.031 --> 00:05:24.949
between the gun manufacturers

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:27.201
and the buyers in these countries.

00:05:29.412 --> 00:05:32.790
There are instances where
legally exported American guns

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.667
can be proven to have been used in

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.669
mass killings in foreign countries.

00:05:37.712 --> 00:05:39.380 position:58%
So one of the things started to do is

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:43.551 position:56%
look at the sales of US guns abroad.

00:05:43.551 --> 00:05:45.720
And one of the things that
popped up immediately is

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.638
that Thailand buys more US guns

00:05:47.638 --> 00:05:49.265
than just about any other country.

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:51.434 position:58%
A lot of those guns have come through

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:53.686
this unique program
called the Gun Welfare Program.

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:56.147 position:59%
Thai police didn't have very many guns

00:05:56.147 --> 00:05:59.359
and the guns they did have were
old and didn't work very well.

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:03.696
So they negotiated with
Sig Sauer and other companies

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:06.324
pretty good prices for the guns.

00:06:06.324 --> 00:06:08.409
The police would buy
them as personal guns

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:10.828
and then use them
when they were on the force.

00:06:10.828 --> 00:06:13.164 position:59%
The problem is that warped the market.

00:06:13.164 --> 00:06:15.041 position:56%
So rather than just buy one of them,

00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:16.542 position:66%
people started to buy two or three of them

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.002
and then you have an investment,

00:06:18.002 --> 00:06:19.754
you could sell them.

00:06:19.754 --> 00:06:22.048 position:66%
So these guns sort of flooded the country.

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:23.591
We followed one in particular,

00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:27.345 position:56%
which was a P365 that Sig Sauer sold

00:06:27.345 --> 00:06:30.598
to a policeman in 2021,

00:06:30.598 --> 00:06:34.685
and that policeman was
later fired for using drugs.

00:06:34.685 --> 00:06:37.355
And ended up using that gun

00:06:37.355 --> 00:06:39.399
in the worst mass shooting

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:40.316
in Thailand's history.

00:06:41.609 --> 00:06:43.027
36 people were killed,

00:06:43.027 --> 00:06:44.237 position:56%
it was a small, tiny little village.

00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:45.905
24 of them were children.

00:06:46.656 --> 00:06:49.951
It's not only one of the worst
mass shootings in Thai history,

00:06:49.951 --> 00:06:52.870 position:56%
it's one of the worst
mass shootings in the world, period.

00:06:55.331 --> 00:06:59.043 position:58%
The prospects for reining in
profligate gun exports might be slim,

00:06:59.043 --> 00:07:02.922
but increasing restrictions
could be surprisingly simple.

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:05.550
The president can do it
with the stroke of a pen.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:08.803 position:63%
The whole time regulations were going
through, it was the president’s decision

00:07:08.803 --> 00:07:11.681 position:58%
to no longer regulate certain weapons

00:07:11.681 --> 00:07:14.892
under the US munitions list.

00:07:14.892 --> 00:07:18.062 position:63%
So President Biden could do it tomorrow.

00:07:18.062 --> 00:07:20.273
The political wrath he would face

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:23.192 position:58%
from businesses, from advocacy groups

00:07:23.192 --> 00:07:24.527
would probably be ferocious.

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:26.404
Joe Biden, for example,

00:07:26.404 --> 00:07:28.948 position:61%
made a promise in his election campaign

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:29.657
where he said,

00:07:29.657 --> 00:07:31.826
if I get elected, I will take this

00:07:31.826 --> 00:07:33.703 position:66%
2020 rule change and I'll just reverse it.

00:07:34.495 --> 00:07:35.955
Here we are, in 2023,

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:37.206
he's running for a second term

00:07:37.206 --> 00:07:39.208
and that reversal hasn't happened.

00:07:40.585 --> 00:07:42.086
So even as the rest of the world

00:07:42.086 --> 00:07:45.006 position:61%
is looking with horror at the situation

00:07:45.006 --> 00:07:47.592
with the gun crime and
the mass shootings in the US,

00:07:47.592 --> 00:07:49.552
the US is loosening restrictions

00:07:49.552 --> 00:07:53.306
and exporting more
guns than ever around the world.

